    "narrative": "Today\u2019s lineup is anchored by **Taylor Ward** (71.19) and **Dylan Beavers** (69.52), who provide our highest efficiency marks against a favorable matchup with Zack Littell. **Taylor Ward** benefits immensely from a leadoff assignment and a park factor boost, while **Dylan Beavers** rounds out a high-upside outfield block that maximizes our exposure to a weak SP_xERA. **Jonathan Aranda** (68.94) remains a primary play, as his strong xwOBA combined with a prime #2 hole spot and a +19% boost from the opposing pitcher\u2019s skill profile makes him a core component of our offensive engine. **Daulton Varsho** (66.20) also merits specific attention; beyond his solid power projection, his ability to capitalize on a slower delivery for stolen base opportunities keeps his score firmly in the \"Strong\" category.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involved **Luis Arraez**, whose score of 39.85 fell just below our strict 40.0 \"Zebras Floor.\" While **Luis Arraez** is a perennial batting average stabilizer, he is currently stifled by a difficult matchup against Chris Sale; the combination of the pitcher\u2019s elite skill and the negative platoon disadvantage tipped the optimizer toward benching him in favor of more aggressive volume plays. We are also monitoring the risk associated with **Matt Chapman**, who at 40.76 is barely clearing the floor threshold while facing Sale. His leadoff spot is the only factor keeping him in the lineup, as his raw metrics are heavily suppressed by the SP\u2019s high skill ceiling. With **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** remaining on the IL and **Jeremiah Jackson** still optioned to the minors, we are forced to rely on **Geraldo Perdomo** and **Bryson Stott** to fill the MI/2B slots; both are currently marginal, serving as placeholders until our deeper depth pieces like **Brendan Donovan** or **Gleyber Torres** find more favorable matchups.",
